In this lesson we'll look at how to handle multi-step experiments using tree diagrams and arrays, especially when one event depends on another. Let's start with tree diagrams. Think of a tree as a branching path where each fork represents one possible outcome at a particular step, and the labels on the branches show the probabilities. You multiply along any single path to find the chance of that whole sequence, and when several paths end in the same result, you add those probabilities together. Next, arrays. Imagine a grid with the outcomes of one variable running down the side and the outcomes of the other running across the top, so every cell is a unique combination. Arrays are super handy when both things you're tracking are categories. Finally, the big idea, independent versus dependent. If you're flipping a coin twice, the second toss doesn't care what the first one showed, so the branch probabilities stay the same. But if you're pulling cards without replacement, the second draw depends on what you pulled first, so the branches shrink or shift to match what's left. Keep that replacement rule in mind and you'll be fine. Now let's see it in action.